# Agent lanes

{{OWNER_NAME}} runs more than one AI tool on this repo (for example Cursor Agent, Claude Code, a desktop assistant, scheduled bots). They do not share chat memory. Give each kind of work one owner, and hand off through a file: `{{HANDOFF_FILE}}`.

Edit the table to match your set-up. The shape is what matters: risky side effects get one owner and a human trigger.

| Lane | Owner | Others |
|------|-------|--------|
| Outbound side effects: sending email or chat, calendar invites, sharing documents, publishing a site or ad account change | **{{SIDE_EFFECT_OWNER}}**, only when the user asks in the current message | Everyone else is blocked; write the request into the handoff file |
| Scheduled or housekeeping agents in `.cursor/agents/` | **{{HOUSEKEEPING_OWNER}}** | Others do not launch them unless the user names the agent in the current message |
| Repo coding, refactors, plans, tests, generated config | **{{CODING_OWNER}}** | Others may make small edits; never both editing one set of files in a turn |
| Read-only lookups through MCP servers | Any | No lane. Do not repeat a pass another agent already filed |
| Overnight bots | **{{BOT_OWNER}}** | Read their latest output; never re-run a bot pass |
| Pull request review | {{REVIEW_OWNER}}, once per PR | No second review of the same PR |

## Enforcement

Rules are advice. Back the important lanes with a hook:

- A `PreToolUse` hook that checks the tool name (or subagent name) against a deny list and returns a deny decision. Use deny, not ask: some modes ignore ask.
- A one-use, time-limited pass the user can grant when they do want the blocked action, for example a file with an expiry that the hook checks. Never create a pass on your own initiative.
- Hooks may not fire inside subagents, so block the subagent at launch.

## Handoff

When work moves lanes, add an entry to `{{HANDOFF_FILE}}` (UTC time, agent, goal, paths, links, next owner, blockers) and stop. Say "hand to {{OTHER_AGENT}}" rather than working around a block.
